From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 23:13:26 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 21/24] script: add SVGForeignObjectElement and its getBBox

<foreignObject> had no element type, so it fell back to plain SVGElement
and getBBox was not a function on it. Adds the type and implements
getBBox as the element's declared x/y/width/height, which is what the
spec defines it as: getBBox on a foreignObject reports its rectangle and
does not measure the content inside. So this is the spec answer rather
than a placeholder for one. x/y/width/height are mapped as presentation
attributes, as they already were for <image>, which shares them.

What this does NOT do is lay out the HTML inside. That is the inverse
hinge: an <svg> is replaced content, so nothing below it builds boxes,
and giving foreign content boxes means running normal CSS layout from
inside the SVG subtree and then getting its output into a scene painted
with vello rather than through the display list. getBoundingClientRect
on a div inside a foreignObject correctly reports 0x0.

svg/ WPT: +344 subtests against the pref-off baseline, up from +332.

READ THIS BEFORE ENABLING MERMAID. This changes Mermaid's failure mode
for the worse, and the earlier review in the Copse repo predicted it.
Mermaid used to throw on getBBox and produce nothing, which the app
detected as a failure. It now runs to completion and emits a diagram
whose nodes are correctly positioned -- translate(38,23), (38,103),
(38,183) -- but uniformly 60x30 and unlabelled, because it measures
labels with getBoundingClientRect on HTML inside a foreignObject and
gets 0x0. Rendered, that is three black boxes with no text and no
visible edges.

The review called this "Mermaid would succeed and stack every node on
the same point"; the detail is different -- they are not stacked -- but
the substance was right: a broken diagram rendered instead of an honest
error. The two things are separable, and this commit is on the correct
side of the line for the engine. Whether the *app* should show Mermaid
diagrams under Servo is a different decision, and the answer today is
no.
